# --- Database ---
# In docker-compose the DATABASE_URL is built from these parts (see
# docker-compose.yml) — do NOT set a full DATABASE_URL here, it is ignored.
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD is REQUIRED: compose interpolates it with :? and refuses to
# boot a deployment without a strong value (a known default password on a
# public host is a compromised database, and the role password is baked into
# the volume at first boot). Compose-only vars — no code in src/ or scripts/
# reads these; the consumers are docker-compose.yml itself: the `db` service
# env, its pg_isready healthcheck, and the DATABASE_URL default interpolated
# for every app/worker service.
# NOTE: changing POSTGRES_PASSWORD after first boot does nothing — the
# password is baked into the Postgres role at volume creation.
POSTGRES_USER="securevoice"
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=""
POSTGRES_DB="securevoice"

# Seed the demo case history on first boot (idempotent). Set "false" for a
# clean production database.
SEED_DEMO="true"

# --- Demo enrollment (required for the console "Fire signal" button) ---------
# The hardened /v1/interventions path resolves the call destination from an
# enrolled Customer row, tenant-scoped, and refuses with a typed
# `customer_not_enrolled` when there is none. The seed script does NOT invent a
# number: dialling a number nobody verified is a real call to a real stranger.
# Set BOTH to a trial number you have verified on the Twilio account, then
# `bun run db:seed` once. Without them the console button returns a typed 409 —
# visible and safe, but a dead click during a demo.
DEMO_TEST_PHONE=""
DEMO_CONSENT_RECORD_ID=""

# Full connection string — ONLY for running Prisma/your own tooling outside
# compose (e.g. against an external Postgres). Ignored by the compose stack.
# NOTE: Supabase's DIRECT host is AAAA-only (IPv6); use the IPv4 pooler:
#   postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<pw>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres
#     ?pgbouncer=true&sslmode=require
# Use sslmode=require (not "disable"). src/lib/db.ts refuses to boot in
# production against a URL without it, because the connection carries case rows,
# redacted transcripts and customer phone numbers.
#
# On a private network you control (e.g. the compose stack's internal db
# service), where TLS is genuinely not available, acknowledge that explicitly
# rather than downgrading every deployment:
#   DB_ALLOW_PLAINTEXT_PRIVATE_NETWORK=true
DATABASE_URL=""

# Dev/test ONLY (the VPS build its own URL from POSTGRES_* below and needs no
# sslmode). Against a MANAGED Postgres such as Supabase, use:
#
#   DATABASE_URL="postgresql://...@aws-0-region.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true&prepare_threshold=0&sslmode=verify-full&sslrootcert=supabase-ca.crt"
#
# Three parts of that URL each carry a requirement, and all three are easy to drop
# by accident:
#
#   · port 6543 + pgbouncer=true — TRANSACTION mode. This is what makes a large
#     per-process pool safe: Supavisor multiplexes many logical clients over few
#     backends, so the `max` in src/lib/db-pool.ts is no longer bounded by the
#     pooler's client quota. On 5432 (session mode) it is, and the fleet trips
#     EMAXCONNSESSION. `prepare_threshold=0` disables prepared statements, which
#     transaction mode does not support.
#   · sslmode=verify-full + sslrootcert=supabase-ca.crt — the full chain is
#     validated against that exact root, so a certificate issued by anyone else is
#     refused. Without the anchor every DB-backed suite fails with "self signed
#     certificate in certificate chain" — that message means the local trust store
#     is missing an anchor, NOT that the server is hostile. Do NOT fix it by
#     dropping sslmode: that verifies nothing. See supabase-ca.README.md.
#   · connection_limit — the per-process pool size. Behind transaction mode this
#     can be large (100 is the default in src/lib/db-pool.ts); behind session mode
#     it must stay small. DB_POOL_MAX / DB_AUDIT_POOL_MAX override it.
#
# DIRECT_URL — the UNPOOLED connection, for migrations only. DDL does not work
# through a transaction-mode pooler, so `prisma migrate deploy` must use this.
# The runtime must never use it: it bypasses the pooler and pins a backend per
# connection. Set it to the direct Postgres URL (port 5432).
# DIRECT_URL=""
#
# Master key for the per-case envelope encryption (WP-15, src/lib/privacy/crypto-shred.ts).
# The post-call ingest SEALS the transcript and analysis into the audit chain as
# AES-256-GCM ciphertext under a per-case data key, wrapped by THIS key, and
# clears the plaintext columns — at rest, the readable transcript exists nowhere.
# There is deliberately no default: a published fallback would make every sealed
# payload decryptable from a database dump alone. Accepts 32 bytes as 64 hex
# chars, 32 bytes base64, or a passphrase (scrypt-stretched).
# Without it the ingest stores NO payload at all (fail closed) — the case still
# advances and the drop is audited. Losing this key shreds every case; rotating
# it needs a re-wrap pass that is not implemented.
PRIVACY_MASTER_KEY=""

# Retention policy (src/lib/privacy/retention.ts), enforced by the
# retention-worker compose service every RETENTION_SWEEP_HOURS (default 24 —
# the first sweep runs at boot, so a worker that was down owes the backlog a
# sweep, not a wait). Defaults: audio 30d, transcripts 90d, case records 7y.
# Erasure crypto-shreds the per-case key; the chain itself is append-only.
# RETENTION_SWEEP_HOURS="24"
# PRIVACY_AUDIO_RETENTION_DAYS="30"
# PRIVACY_TRANSCRIPT_RETENTION_DAYS="90"
# PRIVACY_CASE_RECORD_RETENTION_DAYS="2555"
# Orgs under a no-recording agreement: audio deletion is immediate.
# PRIVACY_PILOT_ORG_IDS=""

# --- Environment posture ---
# Explicit, so a pre-production deploy can never silently inherit production's
# permissions. `canContactRealNumbers` is true ONLY for "production".
#   production  = outbound calls + SMS allowed (real customer contact)
#   staging     = same code, same DB, but outbound calls/SMS return 403, so a
#                 pre-prod demo can never reach a real customer's phone
#   development = dry-run voice, no external calls
# Falls back to NODE_ENV, then "development".
# Irreversible fraud actions are unaffected in every environment: card_freeze
# only ever stages a reversible request for a human specialist.
APP_ENV="development"

# --- ElevenLabs Agents Platform (Stage 2) ---
# The real conversational agent that orchrates the fraud-intervention call.
# Created via POST /v1/convai/agents. Browser sessions are minted server-side
# from this id only — /api/elevenlabs/signed-url rejects any other agent_id
# with 403 agent_not_allowed, which is how agent pinning is enforced.
ELEVENLABS_AGENT_ID=""

# Shared secret that the ElevenLabs agent must present on every webhook tool
# call (sent as the `x-agent-tool-secret` header). 32+ random bytes:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
# This is the ONLY credential standing between an untrusted caller and a
# card_freeze request, so it must never be exposed to a browser or logged.
AGENT_TOOL_SECRET=""

# Comma-separated allow-list of tool names this deployment will authorize.
# A valid secret for one tool does NOT authorize another — the tool name must
# also appear here (see src/lib/agent-tool-auth.ts). Keep this minimal:
#   AGENT_TOOL_ALLOWED="card_freeze,human_handoff,verify_transaction,switch_language"
AGENT_TOOL_ALLOWED="card_freeze,human_handoff,verify_transaction,switch_language"

# Where the MCP transport (/api/elevenlabs/mcp) forwards tools/call. This is
# the app's OWN origin and must never be derived from an incoming request:
# the caller controls the Host header (Caddy forwards it verbatim), so a
# request-derived origin would send the tool credential to the caller's own
# server. The default (http://127.0.0.1:$PORT) is correct for a single-process
# deployment and for the standalone server; set the compose service name only
# when the MCP route runs somewhere that cannot reach itself on localhost.
#   MCP_TOOL_FORWARD_ORIGIN="http://app:3000"
MCP_TOOL_FORWARD_ORIGIN=""

# ElevenLabs platform resource ids applied by scripts/agent-apply.ts — the tool
# endpoints behind AGENT_TOOL_ALLOWED and the per-language knowledge bases.
# WHO READS THESE: scripts/agent-apply.ts, resolving the `id_env:` entries in
# agent/securevoice.agent.yaml (and scripts/integrations-check.mjs reports the
# TOOL ids). No server code path reads them — the agent's tools/KBs are bound
# at apply time, so setting one here without running agent-apply changes nothing.
ELEVENLABS_TOOL_CARD_FREEZE=""
ELEVENLABS_TOOL_HUMAN_HANDOFF=""
ELEVENLABS_TOOL_WARM_TRANSFER=""
ELEVENLABS_TOOL_VERIFY_TRANSACTION=""
ELEVENLABS_TOOL_SWITCH_LANGUAGE=""
# The policy the agent cites, one document id per spoken language. Six, because
# the agent answers in six: an Arabic call quoting a policy that exists only in
# English is a retrieval exercise, not a disclosure. The TEXT lives in the repo at
# agent/policy/SV-FDP-2026-01.<lang>.md — these are the platform ids that copy was
# published under, and tests/unit/knowledge-language-coverage.test.ts fails if a
# language has a file without an entry here or an entry without a file.
ELEVENLABS_KB_EN=""
ELEVENLABS_KB_AR=""
ELEVENLABS_KB_HI=""
ELEVENLABS_KB_UR=""
ELEVENLABS_KB_FR=""
ELEVENLABS_KB_SW=""

# Live warm-transfer destination for the warm_transfer tool: the number Twilio
# dials to bridge the customer to a human mid-call. Per-tenant override:
# "transferPhone" in the org's metadata JSON (validated E.164). Empty = the
# tool degrades to the specialist-queue semantics instead of a live bridge.
HUMAN_AGENT_PHONE=""

# --- ElevenLabs Integration ---
# The team key — server-side ONLY. Never echoed to clients. Read once at boot,
# held in process memory, never logged.
#
# To get a key: app.elevenlabs.io â†’ Profile + API Key
# Rotation cadence: every 90 days. Old keys stay valid for 7d after rotation
# to allow zero-downtime cutover.
ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=""

# Multi-key pool (src/lib/elevenlabs/pool.ts). ELEVENLABS_API_KEY stays the
# `primary`; ELEVENLABS_KEY_1 … ELEVENLABS_KEY_8 are additional keys the pool
# reads and rotates through round-robin, so a 429/422 on one key does not kill
# every caller's audio. Same server-side-only semantics as the key above.
# ELEVENLABS_KEY_1=""
# ELEVENLABS_KEY_2=""
# Parking durations when a key fails, ms, read per failure (defaults shown;
# any finite non-negative number wins, anything else takes the default):
# POOL_RATE_LIMIT_COOLDOWN_MS="60000"       # 429 rate-limited
# POOL_CREDITS_COOLDOWN_MS="21600000"       # 422 out of credits (6h)
# POOL_UNAUTHORIZED_COOLDOWN_MS="3600000"   # 401/403 revoked or wrong key (1h)
# POOL_TRANSIENT_COOLDOWN_MS="5000"         # network/5xx (5s)
# POOL_MAX_RETRIES_PER_KEY="2"              # per-key retry cap (default 2 for a
#                                           # one-key pool, 0 when pooling)

# Used ONCE, at seed time, by `bun run db:seed:audio`
# (scripts/seed-demo-audio.mjs) to synthesise the five playable demo recordings
# under public/demo-audio/. Idempotent — existing files are skipped. Without a key
# it writes nothing and the dashboard's Recording column shows the honest
# "sealed" chip rather than a play button that produces silence.
# DEMO_VOICE_EN=""   # optional per-language voice override
# DEMO_VOICE_AR=""

# Vendor mode. When "true", all upstream calls are skipped and the existing
# z-ai-web-dev-sdk dev backend serves the request. This is the default for
# the public demo so we never burn quota. Flip to "false" (or remove) for
# the Stage 2 build to use the real ElevenLabs platform.
ELEVENLABS_DRY_RUN="true"

# The phone number that PLACES the outbound dial — the caller ID the customer
# sees. Import it once (or "Import number" in the Agents dashboard):
#   POST https://api.elevenlabs.io/v1/convai/phone-numbers
#     {"provider":"sip_trunk","label":"bank-main","phone_number":"+971…",
#      "outbound_trunk_config":{"address":"<institution SBC host>"}}
# The response is {"phone_number_id":"…"} — paste that here. With provider
# "sip_trunk" the DID rides the institution's OWN trunk, so the customer sees
# the same number their fraud team calls from (docs/TRUST-MODEL.md, Layer 1 —
# genuinely theirs, never rented). GET /v1/convai/phone-numbers lists what the
# workspace holds. The dial refuses to place a call while this is unset
# (src/lib/elevenlabs/outbound-call.ts).
ELEVENLABS_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=""

# Account-level ceilings enforced by src/lib/elevenlabs/egress.ts, the single
# guard every upstream ElevenLabs call passes through. Both are read per call,
# so they can be tightened without a rebuild.
#
# ELEVENLABS_MONTHLY_CHAR_LIMIT: characters of synthesis the SHARED platform key
# may bill in a calendar month. The demo tier is 10,000; the guard refuses past
# it with 429 + Retry-After to the next cycle, and refunds any reservation the
# vendor rejected. An org's own BYOK key is not charged against this.
#
# ELEVENLABS_EGRESS_PER_HOUR: requests per hour per caller allowed to reach the
# vendor at all. Refused before the request is sent, so a scrape loop spends
# nothing.
# ELEVENLABS_MONTHLY_CHAR_LIMIT="10000"
# ELEVENLABS_EGRESS_PER_HOUR="120"
# Retry ladder for vendor requests: max attempts, backoff seed, per-attempt cap.
# ELEVENLABS_BACKOFF_MS=""
# ELEVENLABS_MAX_RETRIES=""
# ELEVENLABS_MAX_RETRY_WAIT_MS=""

# Commercial-use attestation. ElevenLabs Terms §1(c)(i) permit a Free User to
# use the services for NON-COMMERCIAL purposes only, and PUP §9(a) lists
# commercial use on a free account as prohibited. A bank fraud-intervention
# pilot is commercial.
#
# The egress guard refuses every live vendor call unless this is exactly "true",
# so it cannot be forgotten at a call site. Set it only once the account is on a
# plan that permits commercial use — the guard will not tell you whether the
# upgrade actually happened.
# ELEVENLABS_COMMERCIAL_USE="true"

# Durable backing for the monthly character meter above. docker-compose defaults
# this to redis://redis:6379 (the `redis` service is already defined there), so
# set it only to point somewhere else or to opt out.
#
# With REDIS_URL unset the counter is per-process: it resets on every restart, so
# it cannot hold a MONTHLY ceiling across a redeploy. The guard says so in the
# log ("degraded: in-process budget limit active") rather than implying it is
# durable, and applies a tightened ceiling while degraded.
#
# In production the connection must be encrypted — use rediss:// (e.g. a managed
# Redis' TLS endpoint). A plaintext redis:// is refused in production unless you
# acknowledge a private network you control:
#   REDIS_ALLOW_PLAINTEXT_PRIVATE_NETWORK="true"
# (docker-compose sets this for its internal redis service.)
# REDIS_URL="rediss://default:PASSWORD@cache.example.com:6379"

# ElevenLabs model overrides (only take effect when DRY_RUN != "true").
# Keep ELEVENLABS_MODEL equal to `tts.model_id` in agent/securevoice.agent.yaml.
# tests/unit/tts-model-resolution.test.ts asserts that equality per language,
# because the console speaking eleven_multilingual_v2 while the live agent spoke
# eleven_v4_turbo made one sentence sound like two different products.
# eleven_v3 is NOT available in the Conversational-AI agent plane on this account
# (PATCH rejects it with 400 expressive_tts_not_allowed on every key tested
# 2026-10-09), so pinning v3 here would let the console synthesize and the dial fail.
ELEVENLABS_MODEL="eleven_v4_turbo"
ELEVENLABS_STT_MODEL="scribe_v2"
# Per-language TTS model pin (model generations carry different language sets —
# see the voice-routing note on ur/sw below).
# ELEVENLABS_TTS_MODEL_UR=""
# ELEVENLABS_TTS_MODEL_SW=""

# Deepgram nova-2 — independent ASR fallback vendor (used when ElevenLabs STT
# is unconfigured or errors). Server-side only.
DEEPGRAM_API_KEY=""
# Hard wall-clock cap on a call served by the media-stream worker, in seconds.
# Defaults to 180 and must match `max_duration_seconds` in
# agent/securevoice.agent.yaml — a gate (tests/unit/voice-call-hard-cap.test.ts)
# fails if the two planes drift apart. Clamped to 30-600: a larger value would let
# a fraud call occupy a billed line indefinitely, a smaller one would cut off the
# opening disclosure. Unset is the correct setting; it means "the declared
# invariant".
# VOICE_MAX_CALL_SECONDS=180
# Endpoint host, model pin and request timeout for the Deepgram path (defaults:
# https://api.deepgram.com/v1, nova-2, 30s). ASR_TIMEOUT_MS caps the parallel
# dev-backend ASR race separately.
# DEEPGRAM_BASE_URL="https://api.deepgram.com/v1"
# DEEPGRAM_STT_MODEL="nova-2"
# DEEPGRAM_TIMEOUT_MS="30000"
# ASR_TIMEOUT_MS="30000"
# Trailing silence (ms) Deepgram waits after the caller's last syllable before it
# sends the UtteranceEnd message on the LIVE listen socket. The span
# `caller_stop_to_agent_audio` starts at that syllable, so this knob is what makes
# the measurement exist. Clamped to 1000-10000 and defaulted to 1000 — Deepgram's
# own floor, and the message requires interim_results=true which the URL always
# sends. It cannot be 0: 0 is Deepgram's "never send it", which would switch a
# published latency budget off while every code path still looked instrumented.
# DEEPGRAM_UTTERANCE_END_MS="1000"
# Ceiling (ms) on a believable caller-stop→agent-audio sample, clamped to
# 1000-60000. The span joins Deepgram's audio-offset clock to this process's wall
# clock, so a stale anchor (a reconnect mid-utterance) can produce a gap of
# minutes; those samples are REFUSED and logged at error level rather than
# recorded, and this is the ceiling that decides "refused".
# VOICE_CALLER_STOP_MAX_MS="15000"

# --- Voice routing (prod mode) ---
# Real ElevenLabs voice_ids, one per SUPPORTED_LANGS language
# (en|ar|hi|ur|fr|sw — six, not four). Required when ELEVENLABS_DRY_RUN != "true":
# the /api/tts route accepts a language key and resolves it to the voice below.
# Cloned bank-approved voices should be registered here AND in the Voice table
# (consent metadata is enforced server-side for clones).
#
# WHAT IS LIVE TODAY, stated plainly rather than as an aspiration: the six ids in
# the deployment's .env are ElevenLabs PREMADE voices labelled `language: en`
# (Sarah/George/Alice/Brian/Roger/Matilda). The words are in the right language —
# measured 2026-10-13 by synthesizing each language and transcribing it back with
# Scribe, which returned the intended text for en/ar/fr/sw and near-exact for hi/ur
# — but they are spoken in an English speaker's voice. Nothing here can detect that:
# `GET /v1/voices?supported_languages=ar` returns this account's voices with EMPTY
# supported_languages arrays, so the vendor offers no per-language capability
# metadata to gate against. Replacing them with cloned native voices is a bank
# decision (consent, and which voice represents the brand), not an API lookup.
# ELEVENLABS_VOICE_EN=""
# ELEVENLABS_VOICE_UR is load-bearing, not a nice-to-have. The Governed
# Collections brief names Urdu explicitly ("from Emirati Arabic to Urdu"), and an
# empty value here makes the Urdu path fail at SYNTHESIS — the request goes out
# with an empty voice_id and comes back as no audio, which is silence on a
# collections call. Nothing upstream warns you: the language still resolves, the
# agent still routes, and every English test still passes. If you are deploying
# for a UAE customer base, fill this in before the first live call.
ELEVENLABS_VOICE_EN=""
ELEVENLABS_VOICE_AR=""
ELEVENLABS_VOICE_HI=""
ELEVENLABS_VOICE_UR=""
ELEVENLABS_VOICE_FR=""
# Urdu and Swahili are NOT carried by eleven_multilingual_v2 (29 languages) or
# by eleven_flash_v2_5 (those same 29 plus hu/no/vi). Both are carried by
# `eleven_v4_turbo`, which is what src/lib/elevenlabs/client.ts pins them to by
# default — verified 2026-10-09: direct synthesis returned 200 audio for ur and
# sw. This note previously claimed the v3 generation; v3 synthesis works on a
# capable key but the Conversational-AI agent plane refuses it on this account.
# Override per language with ELEVENLABS_TTS_MODEL_UR / ELEVENLABS_TTS_MODEL_SW.
ELEVENLABS_VOICE_SW=""

# The second voice for the scripted demo's CUSTOMER line. On a live call the
# customer is a human, so this exists only to make a demo listenable — and it is
# deliberately not one of the six above. Empty means the customer line uses the
# same language's agent voice, which is honest. The demo used to carry its own
# voice table instead, and it silently borrowed another language's production
# slot: an English demo's customer spoke in the Hindi voice, showing a bank
# operator a voice they would never hear on a call.
ELEVENLABS_VOICE_DEMO_CUSTOMER=""
#
# ONE IDENTITY, IF THAT IS THE DECISION. These six slots are per language because a
# deployment may need them to be, not because the product must change speakers.
# Put the same voice id in all six and the agent is one person across six
# languages — on the live call and, because the demo now resolves through the same
# table, in every demo and quick interaction too. Choosing that id is a listening
# decision: `bun scripts/voice-audition.mjs <voice id|name>` renders the real
# opening disclosure in all six languages for each candidate. A cloned voice must
# ALSO be registered as a `Voice` row with consentUrl/consentAt — cloning is
# disabled on this account, and which voice represents the brand is a bank-facing
# decision, not an API lookup.

# --- Rate limits ---
# Per-caller budget for TTS / ASR / agent turns. Default 60/hour.
RATE_LIMIT_PER_HOUR="60"

# --- Vendor API base URLs ---
# Every upstream vendor host is overridable, so a gateway/proxy can sit in front,
# and a region move is a config change, not a rebuild. Leave unset for the
# vendor's default.
# ELEVENLABS_API_BASE_URL=""    # default https://api.elevenlabs.io
# GROQ_BASE_URL=""              # default https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/chat/completions
# GEMINI_BASE_URL=""            # default https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/openai/chat/completions

# --- Timeouts, limits, and business parameters ---
# All read per-call from env so they can be tuned without a rebuild. Defaults
# are conservative; set them from the real vendor/account pages before any load.
#
# TTS / voice
# ELEVENLABS_TTS_TIMEOUT_MS="25000"     # per-synthesis timeout (default 25s)
# ELEVENLABS_SIGNED_URL_TTL_SECONDS="900"  # signed WebSocket URL TTL (default 900; vendor max)
# DAILY_CHAR_LIMIT="2000"               # per-caller daily synthesis chars
# MAX_TTS_CHARS="1024"                  # per-request char limit
#
# Anonymous demo callers. 0 (default) refuses a signed-out caller with 401 and the
# browser's own SpeechSynthesis speaks the line — which is exactly what the public
# demo did before this knob existed, while the page claimed a neural voice.
# Setting it above zero lets an anonymous visitor synthesize on the PLATFORM key up
# to this many characters per HOUR, per client IP, charged in characters (so many
# short requests cannot outrun it) and enforced identically on /api/tts and
# /api/tts/stream. It is hourly because that is what the limiter enforces and a
# signed-out caller has no row to hold a daily counter; it bounds what one scraped
# visitor can drain, not what the demo costs in aggregate. 2000 ≈ twenty demo lines.
# TTS_ANON_CHAR_LIMIT_PER_HOUR="0"
# TTS_CACHE_TTL_MS="3600000"            # TTS cache TTL (1h)
# TTS_CACHE_MAX="96"                    # TTS cache entry cap
# TTS_VOICE_STABILITY="0.5"             # ElevenLabs voice stability [0-1]
# TTS_VOICE_SIMILARITY_BOOST="0.75"     # ElevenLabs similarity boost [0-1]
# TTS_VOICE_USE_SPEAKER_BOOST="true"    # ElevenLabs speaker boost
# TTS_DEFAULT_VOICE_SPEED="1.0"         # Default voice speed multiplier
# TTS_REQUEST_BUDGET_MS="10000"         # hard wall-clock cap on the WHOLE
#                                       # fallback ladder for one request (the
#                                       # per-attempt timeouts multiply; this
#                                       # bounds the sum — src/lib/config.ts
#                                       # ttsRequestBudgetMs, default 10s)
#
# PlayHT — the independent rung after ElevenLabs egress and the key pool
# (src/lib/elevenlabs/playht.ts). Unset API key/user id = the rung refuses and
# the ladder falls to emergency audio; every value below is read per call.
# PLAYHT_API_KEY=""                     # required to arm the rung at all
# PLAYHT_USER_ID=""                     # required with the key (X-USER-ID)
# PLAYHT_VOICE_ID=""                    # voice to synthesise with
# PLAYHT_COMMERCIAL_USE=""              # "true" attestation; anything else =
#                                       # the rung refuses (same rule as
#                                       # ELEVENLABS_COMMERCIAL_USE)
# PLAYHT_API_BASE_URL="https://api.play.ht"   # gateway/proxy override
# PLAYHT_VOICE_ENGINE="PlayHT2.0"       # engine pin
# PLAYHT_OUTPUT_FORMAT="mp3"            # mp3 | opus | flac | wav
# PLAYHT_QUALITY="medium"               # draft | standard | expert | medium
# PLAYHT_SAMPLE_RATE="24000"            # Hz (>0)
# PLAYHT_TIMEOUT_MS="25000"             # per attempt (>0)
# PLAYHT_EGRESS_PER_HOUR="120"          # per-caller throttle to the vendor
# PLAYHT_MAX_RETRIES="2"                # attempts per call (>=0)
# PLAYHT_MAX_RETRY_WAIT_MS="10000"      # backoff ceiling
# PLAYHT_BREAKER_THRESHOLD="5"          # consecutive failures to open (>=1)
# PLAYHT_BREAKER_OPEN_MS="15000"        # how long the breaker stays open
#
# ASR
# MAX_ASR_B64_CHARS="24000000"          # ~18 MB raw audio (base64)
# MAX_ASR_BODY_BYTES="34000000"         # max request body bytes
#
# Agent / LLM
# MAX_AGENT_WORDS="50"                  # max words per agent reply
# MAX_AGENT_TEXT_CHARS="600"            # max chars per agent reply
# GROQ_TIMEOUT_MS="8000"                # LLM reply timeout (default 8s)
# GROQ_TEMPERATURE="0.3"                # LLM creativity [0-1]
# GROQ_MAX_TOKENS="160"                 # LLM max output tokens
# GEMINI_TIMEOUT_MS="15000"             # the same trio for the Gemini plane
# GEMINI_TEMPERATURE="0.3"              # (src/lib/adapters/llm-adapter.ts
# GEMINI_MAX_TOKENS="512"               #  provider config, not the Groq voice path)
#
# THE LLM TURN BUDGET AND THE KNOWLEDGE BASE
# Failover needs a ceiling on the TURN, not only on the call: each provider
# carries its own timeout, so a chain of them is unbounded without this. An
# attempt is never STARTED if the budget has already gone — the alternative is
# eleven seconds of dead air on the phone instead of the scripted reply.
# LLM_TURN_BUDGET_MS="1500"             # total wall clock for provider attempts
# How long a live turn may wait for document retrieval before answering WITHOUT
# it. Small on purpose: the call holds a ~2s first-audio window, and a policy
# paragraph found in 900ms costs the customer silence to buy a better sentence.
# RAG_RETRIEVAL_TIMEOUT_MS="400"        # knowledge-base deadline per turn
# Ceiling on retrieved characters in one prompt, so one enormous chunk cannot
# crowd the security rules out of the system prompt they are meant to govern.
# RAG_CONTEXT_MAX_CHARS="1200"          # retrieved text per turn
#
# Intervention / SLA
# SLA_SECONDS="60"                      # contact must start within N seconds
# INTERVENTION_CURRENCY="AED"           # ISO-4217 currency for signals
# MINOR_UNITS_PER_MAJOR="100"           # minor units per major (e.g. 100 fils)
# PRENOTIF_LEAD_SECONDS="0"             # head start of the pre-notification SMS
#                                       # before the ring (src/lib/prenotify.ts;
#                                       # the runbook's guidance is 60-90; 0 = off)
# SLA_SWEEP_LIMIT="100"                 # cases per worker tick in the SLA-breach
#                                       # sweep (src/lib/sla-breach.ts)
# CALL_TURN_IDLE_MS="1800000"           # a call with no turn for this long counts
#                                       # as ended for the turn counter
#                                       # (src/lib/voice/turn-counter.ts)
# AUTH_FAILED_LOGIN_MAX="5"             # failed sign-ins per identifier before
#                                       # the lockout in the auth route
# KB_ORG_MAX_BYTES="134217728"          # uploaded-document quota per org
#                                       # (default 128 MiB = sixteen files at the
#                                       # per-file max; src/lib/knowledge/documents.ts)
# LINE_TYPE_LOOKUP_URL=""               # carrier line-type provider endpoint
# LINE_TYPE_LOOKUP_TOKEN=""             # its bearer token; unset = unauthenticated
#                                       # (both unset = no lookup, src/lib/voice/
#                                       # line-type.ts)
#
# Idempotency / replay
# REPLAY_WINDOW_SEC="300"               # webhook timestamp replay window (also
#                                       # read by mini-services/realtime and the
#                                       # scripts/verify_sv_signature.* references)
# IDEMPOTENCY_TTL_HOURS="24"            # idempotency key TTL
# IDEMPOTENCY_CLAIM_TTL_MS="30000"      # claim lease duration
# IDEMPOTENCY_POLL_MS="200"             # poll interval for async ops
# IDEMPOTENCY_MAX_POLL_MS="8000"        # max poll duration
#
# Outbox / callbacks
# APP_BASE_URL="https://app.example.com"     # dev fallback for outbound callback URL
# BANK_WEBHOOK_URL="https://bank.example.com/hooks/securevoice"  # production outbound webhook base URL
# OUTBOX_MAX_ATTEMPTS="6"                    # max delivery attempts before DEAD
# OUTBOX_BACKOFF_MS="60000,300000,1800000,7200000,10800000,43200000"  # retry ladder (ms)
# OUTBOX_DELIVERY_TIMEOUT_MS="10000"         # per-attempt HTTP timeout
#
# ── Runtime tunables (src/lib/config.ts — every default is safe; override per
#    deployment). Grouped by subsystem.
#
# Dial / interventions
# DIAL_PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS="30000"         # live dial probe timeout (30s)
# DIAL_RETRY_LADDER_MS="150000,300000,600000"  # how long a failed dial waits
#    before each next attempt, ms per rung (read by env.dialRetryLadderMs →
#    src/lib/scale/queue.ts; those ARE the defaults, nothing changes until set).
#    Carrier rule, enforced: a number re-dialled inside 5 minutes is flagged as
#    robocall spam, so with the ±20% jitter the first two rungs must satisfy
#    0.8 × (L1+L2) > CALL_RATE_WINDOW_MS. A ladder that breaks the rule (or is
#    not a list of positive ms numbers) is REFUSED — the default applies and a
#    console.warn at boot names the value. Fewer rungs than the 3 attempts is
#    legal: the last rung repeats.
# REVERSAL_WINDOW_SECS="86400"          # how long a staged card hold waits for a
#    second actor before the worker escalates it as lapsed (it is never reversed:
#    nothing is frozen at a bank — src/lib/freeze-window.ts). 24h matches what
#    /llms.txt publishes; the sentence is rendered from this value, so setting a
#    different window cannot leave the public claim behind.
#
# Realtime push
# REALTIME_NOTIFY_TIMEOUT_MS="1500"     # fan-out budget per notify
# REALTIME_TOKEN_TTL_SEC="60"           # grant-token lifetime; MUST match
#                                       # mini-services/realtime (both sides read it)
#
# Realtime client (inlined into the browser bundle)
# NEXT_PUBLIC_REALTIME_TOKEN_TIMEOUT_MS="4000"
# NEXT_PUBLIC_REALTIME_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS="6000"
# NEXT_PUBLIC_REALTIME_RECONNECT_BASE_MS="500"    # backoff seed, doubles per
# NEXT_PUBLIC_REALTIME_RECONNECT_MAX_MS="30000"   # attempt, clamped to this
# NEXT_PUBLIC_REALTIME_MAX_ATTEMPTS="8"           # then the console stays on SSE
#
# CRM adapters
# CRM_HTTP_TIMEOUT_MS="3000"
# CRM_RETRY_DELAY_MS="500"
#
# Database & failure handling
# DB_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS="10000"
# DB_TX_RETRIES="3"
# DB_TX_BACKOFF_MS="25"
# DB_TIMEOUT_RETRY_AFTER_SEC="5"
# REPLICA_LAG_THRESHOLD_MS="2000"
# BREAKER_OPEN_MS="30000"
#
# Rate limits (housekeeping + pilot intake)
# RATE_LIMIT_STALE_MS="7200000"
# PILOT_RATE_LIMIT_PER_HOUR="6"
#
# Readiness warnings
# READYZ_OUTBOX_WARN_SEC="300"
# READYZ_AUDIT_STALE_SEC="3600"
#
# SMS verdict
# SMS_REPLY_WINDOW_MS="86400000"        # SMS reply acceptance window (24h)
#
# Queue & scale model
# QUEUE_LEASE_MS="60000"
# CALL_RATE_WINDOW_MS="300000"
# QUEUE_BACKOFF_BASE_MS="250"
# QUEUE_BACKOFF_MAX_MS="8000"
# MEAN_CALL_SECONDS="180"
#
# Capacity & admission bands (SHED_EXPECTED_LOSS_MINOR is documented below)
# ELEVENLABS_BURST_MULTIPLIER="3"
# BAND_ENTER_CONSTRAINED_PCT="0.7"
# BAND_ENTER_SHED_PCT="0.95"
#
# Auth session policy (src/lib/auth/session-policy.ts)
# AUTH_SESSION_LIFETIME_SEC="28800"     # absolute lifetime (8h)
# AUTH_IDLE_TIMEOUT_SEC="900"           # idle logout (15 min)
# AUTH_COOKIE_CACHE_SEC="300"
# AUTH_STEP_UP_WINDOW_SEC="300"
# AUTH_INVITE_TTL_MS="259200000"        # 72h
# AUTH_MAGIC_LINK_TTL_MS="900000"       # 15 min
# NEXT_PUBLIC_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS="900000"  # client idle timer; keep equal to
#                                       # AUTH_IDLE_TIMEOUT_SEC — the client
#                                       # bundle cannot read the server value
#
# Compliance / tooling odds and ends
# TTS_QUOTA_RETRY_AFTER_SEC="3600"
# CONFORMANCE_PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS="5000"
# TELEMETRY_FLUSH_DEBOUNCE_MS="50"
# RETENTION_REPORT_CAP="50"
# FAILURE_DOCS_BASE_URL=""              # default baked in src/lib/failures/envelope.ts

# --- Feature flags ---
# Every runtime switch lives in src/lib/flags.ts. Values are strictly "true" or
# "false" — anything else (yes, 1, empty) throws at read time, because a typo'd
# value would otherwise read as "disabled" and look like a deliberate choice.
#
# Defaults put the safe path first: anything touching money, PII, or the audit
# chain defaults ON so a blank config is a secure deployment, not an open one.
#
# FEATURE_REALTIME="false"              # signed push to the realtime service (needs
#                                       # REALTIME_INGEST_SECRET too; both or neither)
# FEATURE_CONSOLE_LIVE_FEED="false"     # console live feed over websocket instead of
#                                       # SSE; read by the browser via
#                                       # /api/console/features
# FEATURE_ELEVEN_LABS_LIVE="false"      # real neural voice. ELEVENLABS_DRY_RUN still
#                                       # wins if set, since the provider client reads
#                                       # it directly.
# FEATURE_COMPLIANCE_DISCLOSURE="true"  # "this call is recorded" disclosure. Required
#                                       # by law; defaults ON.
# FEATURE_COMPLIANCE_NO_SECRETS="true"  # never request PIN/password/OTP/CVV. Required
#                                       # by policy; defaults ON.
# FEATURE_PII_REDACTION="true"          # redact PII in logs, audit rows, webhooks.
#                                       # Off = writing customer transcripts in the
#                                       # clear. Debug-only.
# FEATURE_COMPLIANCE_PII_REDACTION="true"  # second (defaults-ON) redaction switch
#                                          # for the outbound wire; no legacy var
#                                          # backs this one.
# FEATURE_WEBHOOK_STRICT_SIGNATURES="true"  # refuse unsigned inbound signals. Defaults
#                                           # ON; OFF is a local-debug affordance only.
# FEATURE_SEED_DEMO="false"             # seed demo case history on first boot.
#                                       # Defaults OFF for a clean production database.

# --- Webhook signing (production) ---
# Server-side secret used BOTH ways:
#  1. Signing outbound post-call webhooks — the customer verifies
#     SV-Signature: t=<ts>,v1=<sha256(t.body, WEBHOOK_SECRET)>.
#  2. Verifying INBOUND risk signals on POST /api/interventions — the bank's
#     fraud engine signs with the same scheme. Rotate by setting a new value
#     + alerting all producers.
WEBHOOK_SECRET=""

# --- Better Auth (authentication) ---
# BOTH values are read at RUNTIME ONLY. Nothing here is NEXT_PUBLIC_*: Better Auth
# is entirely server-side, so unlike the removed Clerk setup there is no build-arg
# / inlined-publishable-key step and `docker compose build` needs no auth env.
#
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET signs the session cookie AND encrypts stored credentials.
# MUST be >= 32 characters; below that Better Auth throws at import and every
# request 500s. There is no fallback. Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
# Rotating it logs out every user. Commit a fresh value per environment.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=""
# Public origin of this deployment. BETTER_AUTH_URL is asserted critical at
# boot (assertCriticalConfig) and docker-compose interpolates it. APP_URL /
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL are fallbacks for the same origin at other call sites.
#
# This is the value Better Auth compares a browser's `Origin` against, so a
# leftover localhost here answers every public sign-in with "Invalid origin".
# With NODE_ENV=production that is now a BOOT failure rather than a broken
# button: the process refuses to come up and the message names this variable.
# The apex/www sibling of whatever you set here is trusted automatically.
#
# THE DEFAULT IS THE PRODUCTION HOST, not a local one, and that ordering is
# deliberate: the VPS reads this file, so the safe value is the one that ships
# when nobody edits it. A forgotten line used to leave a public deployment
# unable to sign anybody in. `docker-compose.yml` and the Dockerfile build arg
# carry the same default, and
# `tests/unit/prod-origin-defaults.test.ts` fails if the three ever disagree.
#
#   Running the compose stack on your own machine? Override it, or every
#   sign-in from http://localhost:3000 is refused as an invalid origin:
#       BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000
BETTER_AUTH_URL="https://securevoiceai.me"
# Extra origins allowed to send credential requests — for a console on another
# host, a status page, or a tunnel. Comma, semicolon, or space separated.
# Each entry must be `https://host` or `https://host:port` with NO path and NO
# wildcard: a wildcard is refused, because an allowlist that matches every
# origin is not an allowlist. Unusable entries are dropped with a startup error
# naming them, and are never trusted.
# BETTER_AUTH_TRUSTED_ORIGINS="https://console.securevoiceai.me"
# APP_URL=""
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=""

# ── Email (Resend) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The mail provider for the three transactional flows: magic-link sign-in
# (PRIMARY), workspace invites, and Better Auth password reset. Without
# RESEND_API_KEY the magic-link endpoint REFUSES (issueMagicLink has no
# deliverer and will not hand back a token) — sign-in falls back to password
# only. See src/lib/email/resend.ts.
#
# The app uses Resend's HTTPS API, not SMTP. smtp.resend.com:465 / 2465 / 587 /
# 2587 with this same key as the password would work, but it needs a
# long-lived socket and a local retry spool, which does not belong in a
# horizontally-scaled app behind Caddy.
#
# Never commit a real key. .gitignore excludes `.env*` and allows only the
# examples.
RESEND_API_KEY=""
# From-address. Use a domain YOU control, not the shared onboarding@resend.dev:
# this mail is a security credential to a bank console, so SPF/DKIM/DMARC must
# align on your own domain or corporate filters will quarantine it. Requires
# the Resend DNS records (SPF include, DKIM CNAME, DMARC) for that domain.
# RESEND_FROM_EMAIL="SecureVoice AI <support@securevoiceai.me>"
# Where bounces and replies go. A no-reply address nobody monitors means a
# silently broken domain. Falls back to NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPPORT_EMAIL, then to
# support@securevoiceai.me.
# RESEND_REPLY_TO="support@securevoiceai.me"
#
# Where pilot / signup requests land.
# Every request to /api/pilot stores the enquiry FIRST and then queues a
# notification to this address, so a mail outage never turns a submitted form
# into a 500 and never loses a lead. The notification goes through the durable
# outbox, so a provider outage retries it.
#
# Set this to the mailbox that should receive them. If it is left unset the
# enquiry is still stored and still appears in the operator console — only the
# email is skipped. A wrong address is not silent: the outbox bounce handler
# marks that message DEAD and logs the reason.
PILOT_NOTIFY_EMAIL="founder@securevoiceai.me"
#
# ── Receiving mail + delivery signals ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Resend → Webhooks → add an endpoint at https://<host>/api/email/inbound and
# paste its signing secret here. Two families of event arrive at that one URL:
#
#   email.received                            → inbound mail to support@/legal@/
#                                               founder@, stored in EmailInbound
#                                               and shown in the console inbox
#   email.bounced / email.complained          → the EmailOutbox row for that send
#   email.delivery_delayed / email.delivered    is marked DEAD (bounce/complaint)
#                                               or annotated, matched by
#                                               providerMessageId
#
# Subscribe to all five. `email.received` alone gives the inbox; without the
# other four a hard bounce is invisible and the retry ladder keeps mailing a
# dead address, which is how a sending domain loses its reputation.
#
# Svix scheme: signed content is `${svix-id}.${svix-timestamp}.${rawBody}`, key
# is this value base64-decoded after the `whsec_` prefix, with a 5-minute replay
# window. Without the secret the endpoint returns 503 and stores nothing.
# RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""
#
# Once the sending domain is verified (docs/RESEND-DNS.md), swap the from-address
# above and point this at the real mailbox. Sending and replying are different
# things: Resend sends AS the verified domain, and replies route here.
#
# ── Retries ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The request path attempts one synchronous send, so the happy path needs no
# worker. These tune the drain worker (compose service `email-worker`), which
# retries anything that failed — WITHOUT IT a Resend outage costs the operator
# their sign-in link, because the queue would sit PENDING forever.
#   EMAIL_WORKER_POLL_MS="10000"
#   EMAIL_WORKER_BATCH="25"
# THE canonical origin. SET THIS IN PRODUCTION.
#
# This is no longer a nice-to-have. It drives metadataBase in src/app/layout.tsx,
# which in turn resolves every relative path in the metadata tree: the canonical
# URL, the OpenGraph and Twitter image URLs, and the sitemap entries emitted by
# src/app/sitemap.ts. Unset in production, all of those resolve against
# http://localhost:3000 — a sitemap that points at localhost, and a canonical
# tag that tells a crawler the real page is a duplicate of nowhere.
#
# src/lib/site-origin.ts falls back to SITE_ADDRESS promoted to https, then to
# the dev origin, but the explicit value is the one to set: it is a NEXT_PUBLIC_
# var, so the client bundle reads the same answer the server does.
#
# The fallback has one sharp edge worth knowing about, because it cost this
# deployment its search visibility once: on a VPS before the domain is wired,
# the fallback resolves to a stand-in like `<vps-ip>.sslip.io`. A certificate
# from a dynamic-DNS name is fine; a CANONICAL origin from one is not — every
# `<link rel="canonical">`, every `<loc>`
# in the sitemap and the `Sitemap:` line in robots.txt then name a different
# domain from the one visitors reach, and a crawler that is told the real page
# lives elsewhere will not rank this one. `preflight` reports this as NO-GO, and
# `siteOriginIsPlaceholder()` in src/lib/site-origin.ts is the same rule in code.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL="https://securevoiceai.me"
#
# The bare-hostname spelling of the same answer, for an operator who already sets
# SITE_ADDRESS and does not want to compose an `https://` string twice.
# Explicitly setting either one outranks the SITE_ADDRESS fallback.
# SITE_DOMAIN="securevoiceai.me"

# ── Billing (Paddle) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Which PaymentProvider is bound. Unset = no gateway, and /api/billing/* returns
# 503 rather than pretending to take a payment. A gateway is never silently
# defaulted: a billing system that quietly does nothing when misconfigured fails
# on the day a customer is charged nothing.
#   PAYMENT_PROVIDER="paddle"#
# Sandbox keys are prefixed pdl_sdbx_, live keys pdl_live_. The Paddle adapter
# REFUSES a live key unless PADDLE_ALLOW_LIVE_KEYS=1, so a mis-set environment
# cannot take real money. https://sandbox-vendors.paddle.com/authentication-v2
#   PADDLE_API_KEY=""
#   PADDLE_NEGOTIATED_PRODUCT_ID=""   # hidden product negotiated one-off prices hang off;
#     found by name ("Negotiated Terms") and created once if unset.
#   PADDLE_TAX_ID_TYPE="eu_vat"        # tax_identifiers[].type Paddle should validate against
#   PADDLE_BASE_URL=""                 # unset = derived from the key prefix; a disagreeing
#     override is refused rather than billing the other Paddle account
#   PADDLE_ALLOW_LIVE_KEYS=""          # "1" required before a pdl_live_ key is accepted
#   PADDLE_SIGNATURE_TOLERANCE_SEC="300" # webhook ts clock-skew window
#     (src/lib/payments/paddle.ts); widen only for a known-slow receiver.
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_PADDLE_PRICES=""        # INLINED price map the PUBLIC pricing page
#     renders (src/lib/paddle-browser.ts); it THROWS when absent from the bundle,
#     so this must be a build-time value like the demo login above, not just a
#     runtime env. Same "<currency>_<minorUnits>_<interval>" keys as PADDLE_PRICES.
#   PADDLE_SANDBOX_API_KEY=""           # ONLY scripts/paddle-repair-overrides.ts and
#     scripts/seed-paddle-catalog.ts read this name (catalog tooling run by hand);
#     the runtime uses PADDLE_API_KEY above.
#
# The NOTIFICATION SIGNING SECRET (`ntfs_...`), NOT the API key. They are
# different values from different parts of the dashboard. It signs
# `Paddle-Signature: ts=…;h1=…` over `ts:<raw body>`; without it every webhook is
# a digest_mismatch that looks exactly like an attack.
#   PADDLE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""
#
# Price map: "<currency>_<minorUnits>_<interval>" -> "pri_…". The checkout
# resolves a price by AMOUNT, not by price id, so every purchasable plan needs an
# entry or createCheckout throws naming the missing key. 1000 minor USD = $10.00.
# Generate the catalog with: bun run paddle:seed
#   PADDLE_PRICES="{}"
#
# CLIENT side. These are inlined into the browser bundle, so they must be the
# sandbox values while testing. NEXT_PUBLIC_PADDLE_ENVIRONMENT is REFUSED rather
# than defaulted: a wrong environment charges the wrong Paddle account, and
# neither failure announces itself.
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_PADDLE_ENVIRONMENT="sandbox"
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_PADDLE_CLIENT_TOKEN=""    # sandbox tokens are prefixed `test_`
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE=""        # docs quickstart API origin override
# Contact addresses across the public surface (footer, legal, security, billing).
# These are Namecheap Private Email mailboxes on the domain the platform sends
# from. Defaults in src/lib/public-config.ts: support@ / legal@ / founder@
# securevoiceai.me. NOT securevoice.ai, which resolves to an unrelated party.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPPORT_EMAIL=""   # product/billing/security/privacy contact + email reply-to
# NEXT_PUBLIC_LEGAL_EMAIL=""     # DPAs, PDPL/GDPR requests, terms, law enforcement
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FOUNDER_EMAIL=""   # direct line published on the deck/pitch material
# Optional. Enables license-gated Better Auth infra features (dash telemetry).
# The app runs without it.
BETTER_AUTH_API_KEY=""

# --- Session/BYOK secret ---
# 384-bit secret. Encrypts organization BYOK ElevenLabs keys at rest
# (src/lib/byok.ts) — rotating it invalidates stored BYOK keys (re-enter them).
# There is NO fallback: BYOK storage throws without it. Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
AUTH_SECRET=""

# It now also guards the PII vault (src/lib/compliance/pii-vault.ts), which is where
# a customer's number is sealed and indexed:
#   - missing: case creation still works and keeps the number on the row, logged as a
#     degraded write. A fraud alert reaching its customer outranks a seal that cannot
#     be made, so this must never be a hard failure at ingest.
#   - rotated: previously sealed values stop resolving, so a vaulted case loses its
#     dial destination and fails closed rather than dialling a stale number. Vault
#     rows must be re-sealed after a rotation, the same obligation BYOK keys carry.

# --- Capacity ceilings (see src/lib/capacity.ts for provenance) ---
# Vendor ceilings are contracted numbers, not code. Set them from the real
# account pages before any load test; the defaults are deliberately conservative
# so a fresh deployment is throttled rather than surprised.
#
# ElevenLabs concurrent calls, by tier (read from elevenlabs.io/pricing/agents):
#   free 4 · starter 6 · creator 10 · pro 20 · scale 30 · business 40
# Burst pricing ($0.16/min, double the standard $0.08/min) applies above the
# limit; we allow burst up to 3x before shedding.
# ELEVENLABS_MAX_CONCURRENT="4"

# Twilio — account-specific, read from Twilio Console → Voice → Settings.
# Per-FROM-NUMBER calls per second; extra numbers are extra lanes.
# TWILIO_CPS_PER_FROM_NUMBER="2"
# TWILIO_MAX_CONCURRENT_CALLS="10"

# Admission control: expected-loss floor (MINOR UNITS, integer only) below which
# we stop spending the voice channel and fall back to SMS. Default AED 500.
# SHED_EXPECTED_LOSS_MINOR="50000"

# --- Twilio live telephony ---
# Places REAL fraud-intervention calls + SMS to enrolled customers
# (POST /api/enroll). Runtime auth prefers a dedicated API key (rotatable,
# revocable) over the master token. WITHOUT these vars the platform runs in
# audit-only mode — everything works, nothing is dialed.
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=""
TWILIO_API_KEY_SID=""
TWILIO_API_KEY_SECRET=""
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=""
# The caller ID presented on intervention calls. Must be a number the
# institution is authorised to present — their published inbound number, not a
# rented one (docs/TRUST-MODEL.md, Layer 1).
TWILIO_FROM_NUMBER=""
# Adapter-plane sender/URL knobs read by src/lib/adapters/telecom-adapter.ts
# (TELECOM_ADAPTER="twilio" path) as the deployment-wide fallback BELOW the
# per-tenant org-metadata values documented above:
# TWILIO_MESSAGING_SERVICE_SID=""       # account Messaging Service when the
#                                       # tenant has none (From is then omitted)
# TWILIO_CALL_URL=""                    # TwiML callback the adapter points
#                                       # voice calls at
# REST endpoint + per-request timeout (defaults: https://api.twilio.com, 15s).
# Override to front a gateway or pin a region.
# TWILIO_API_BASE_URL="https://api.twilio.com"
# TWILIO_TIMEOUT_MS="15000"
# Signed TwiML audio URL cache TTL (default 1h).
# TWILIO_AUDIO_CACHE_TTL_MS="3600000"
# Live-fire attestation. Credentials are necessary but NOT sufficient: with
# them present, every intervention call and every SMS (pre-notification, blind
# ping, fallback) goes to a real handset the moment a signal arrives. There is
# no sandbox — a fixture that looks like a UAE mobile number is, to Twilio,
# indistinguishable from a customer.
#
# Set to "true" only on a deployment that is meant to reach real people. Left
# unset, src/lib/twilio.ts refuses every request with 403 and names this
# variable, and the test preload deletes it unconditionally so no suite can
# inherit the answer from this file.
# TWILIO_LIVE_SEND="true"
# Inbound SMS replies. When a voice call cannot reach the customer they get a
# "blind ping" text (no merchant, no amount) asking them to reply YES or NO. In the
# Twilio console set that number's "A message comes in" webhook (HTTP POST) to:
#   https://<your-host>/api/sms/inbound
# The route FAILS CLOSED: it needs TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN (an API key alone cannot verify
# inbound webhooks) and rejects anything without a valid X-Twilio-Signature.
# Twilio signs the PUBLIC url it called, so behind a proxy set this to that origin:
# TWILIO_WEBHOOK_BASE_URL="https://app.example.com"
# How often the dial worker runs its deadline sweeps: unanswered 24h SMS cases,
# staged card holds whose reversal window has closed, and the unacknowledged
# escalation ladder. The name is historical — one tick paces all three.
# SMS_SWEEP_EVERY_MS="60000"
# TRIAL ACCOUNTS: calls/SMS only succeed to numbers verified in the Twilio
# console (Phone Numbers â†’ Verified Caller IDs). Upgrade for unrestricted
# delivery. Find credentials: console.twilio.com â†’ Account Info / API Keys.

# --- Multi-tenant telecom identity (BANK / INSURER ISOLATION) ---------------
# The variables above are the PLATFORM's telephony account. They decide nothing
# about which number a customer sees — that is per tenant, in the database, and
# it is what keeps Bank A's alert out of Bank B's name:
#
#   organization.twilioVoiceNumber          the tenant's own number: the caller ID
#                                           on a Twilio voice leg AND the number
#                                           this route matches an inbound call on
#   organization.twilioSmsSenderId          the tenant's SMS sender (a number or an
#                                           approved alphanumeric sender id)
#   organization.twilioMessagingServiceSid  the tenant's Messaging Service. Set it and
#                                           no From is sent at all — the service picks
#                                           the sender and the real number comes back
#                                           on the status callback
#   organization.elevenPhoneNumberId        the tenant's DID imported to the
#                                           conversation platform; the caller ID on
#                                           the primary (agent) plane
#
# A tenant with none of these rides the platform identity above. A tenant with
# them is NEVER silently downgraded to it: if the identity cannot be read, or the
# configured number is malformed, the send is refused and the dial job retries,
# because a mis-attributed alert is invisible in delivery logs and fatal to a B2B
# contract.
#
# Delivery status callback — attach per send automatically when the origin below
# is reachable, and point the account's status callbacks here as well:
#   https://<your-host>/api/twilio/status
# It fails closed on X-Twilio-Signature and only ever updates the telecom outbox;
# it does not move case state.
#
# Inbound voice (the customer calls the institution's fraud line back):
#   https://<your-host>/api/twilio/inbound-voice
# Set that as the "A Call Comes In" webhook on every tenant's twilioVoiceNumber.
# The tenant is identified by the number dialled, the case by the caller, and the
# bridge ladder is: the agent (ELEVENLABS_INBOUND_SIP_URI) → a human line
# (organization metadata transferPhone, else HUMAN_AGENT_PHONE) → a spoken
# acknowledgement in the customer's language, recorded for the duty operator.
#
# The SIP address of the platform agent for INBOUND calls, from the institution's
# SIP trunk setup. Unset is supported: the call then goes to a human line if the
# tenant has one, and otherwise the customer hears the acknowledgement instead of
# being bridged into nothing.
# ELEVENLABS_INBOUND_SIP_URI="sip:agent@sip.elevenlabs.io"
# How long an inbound bridge waits for the far end before giving up (seconds).
# INBOUND_DIAL_TIMEOUT_SEC="20"

# --- Upstash QStash (async dispatch between bank intake and the dial worker) ---
# QStash is OPTIONAL: unset, the bank's signal enqueues directly into the
# Postgres queue and the dial worker drains it; nothing else changes. Set, and
# /api/v1/interventions publishes a small envelope to QStash instead; QStash
# delivers it back to /api/queue/dispatch with a signed Upstash-Signature
# header, which verifies and hands off to the durable Postgres queue.
#
# This is the envelope-layer contract:
#   - /api/queue/dispatch and /api/queue/dead-letter verify Upstash-Signature
#     with BOTH the current and next signing keys, so a key rotation does not
#     cut traffic.
#   - Dead letters land in the DeadLetter table (same row the operator replay
#     console reads), keyed by the envelope's idempotencyKey — retrying a
#     dead-letter writes to the SAME row with attempts incremented.
#   - The envelope carries caseRef, orgId, jobKind, and the handler payload.
#     It does NOT carry the bank's phone number or any transcript content.
# QSTASH_TOKEN=""
# QSTASH_CURRENT_SIGNING_KEY=""
# QSTASH_NEXT_SIGNING_KEY=""
# QSTASH_RETRIES="4"               # max retries for a failed QStash dispatch
# QSTASH_DISPATCH_PATH="/api/queue/dispatch"  # path QStash delivers back to
# QUEUE_PUBLIC_URL=""                          # public origin QStash calls

# --- Sentry (remote error capture, opt-in) ---
# Unset: NO error capture — local dev stdout has a real traceback.
# Set: the instrumentation hook initialises @sentry/nextjs at boot and
# measures how often handlers fail. Sensitive fields are stripped before
# anything leaves the process: never a data escape hatch.
#
# THIS FILE IS COMMITTED. Never paste a real DSN here, even a "public" one: a
# client DSN is a WRITE credential for the project, so anyone who reads it in a
# repository can post events into your error feed and poison the traces your
# on-call reads. Put the real value in `.env`. The shape below is the Better
# Stack Sentry-ingest endpoint; Sentry's own SaaS looks different, and both are
# fine as long as the token stays out of version control.
SENTRY_DSN=""
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=""
# SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT="production"
# SENTRY_TRACES_SAMPLE_RATE="0.1"
# The client-bundle pair (src/instrumentation-client.ts) — inlined at build:
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=""     # default falls back to NODE_ENV
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_TRACES_SAMPLE_RATE="0.1"

# --- Axiom (structured log shipping) ---
# Every `logInfo`/`logWarn`/`logError` record from src/lib/validation/safe-log.ts
# is shipped to Axiom by the sink registered in src/instrumentation.ts
# (src/lib/telemetry/axiom.ts). Set the token and structured logs land in the
# dataset below; leave it unset and the sink is a no-op — records still go to
# stdout, so a self-hosted box with no Axiom account logs normally.
#
# The token is a personal/API token from app.axiom.co → Settings → API tokens.
# It is read server-side only and never reaches a client bundle.
AXIOM_TOKEN=""
# Dataset that receives the events. Defaults to "securevoice", matching the
# ingestion endpoint https://api.axiom.co/v1/datasets/securevoice/ingest.
# AXIOM_DATASET="securevoice"
# API base override (default https://api.axiom.co) — set for a regional endpoint
# (e.g. https://api.eu.axiom.co) or an egress proxy. Not a /v1 path.
# AXIOM_URL=""

# --- Pinecone (vector store — case transcripts AND uploaded policy documents) ---
# Unset: transcripts are NOT sent anywhere. When post-call ingest produces a
# case transcript it is sealed at rest (crypto-shred) and never indexed.
# Configuring a public vector DB for transcript data is a conscious
# decision, not a default.
#
# The SAME index backs the institution knowledge base (Settings → Knowledge,
# /api/console/documents). Without PINECONE_API_KEY + PINECONE_INDEX every
# uploaded document lands on FAILED with the machine-readable reason
# `pinecone_not_configured` — shown in the console next to a Retry button.
# A document only reaches READY when both of these are set.
# PINECONE_API_KEY=""
# PINECONE_INDEX=""
# PINECONE_HOST=""   # OPTIONAL — the SDK resolves the host from the API key.
#                     # Only set this for a non-default project host. Requiring
#                     # it used to make a correct setup silently index nothing.
# PINECONE_NAMESPACE=""             # index namespace override
# PINECONE_EMBEDDING_MODEL="llama-text-embed-v2"
# PINECONE_INDEX_MAX_CHARS="12000"  # max transcript chars per indexed case

# --- pgvector transcript RAG (the THIRD plane) --------------------------------
# Plane one is the document knowledge base (Pinecone, above). Plane two is the
# ElevenLabs agent's own knowledge_base list. Plane three is native pgvector:
# post-call transcripts are embedded with OpenAI and stored as vectors inside
# this database, so an operator can semantically search call transcripts without
# any bank call data leaving the tenant boundary. Gated behind OPENAI_API_KEY
# (documented below) — unset means nothing is embedded and nothing leaves the
# building.
#
# OpenAI text-embedding-3-small produces 1536-dim vectors, matching the
# vector(1536) column in migration 9zz..._transcript_embeddings. Change this
# only with a matching migration, or stored vectors break.
# RAG_EMBEDDING_MODEL="text-embedding-3-small"
# Max characters fed to one embedding call; long transcripts are chunked and the
# tail beyond RAG_EMBEDDING_MAX_CHUNKS is not indexed. For Tier-1 banks with
# long fraud-intervention calls, raise this to 6000-8000; the code default is
# 4000 to keep embedding cost and latency bounded on the default plan.
# RAG_EMBEDDING_MAX_CHARS="4000"
# Max chunks embedded per transcript. A bound so one long call cannot burn the
# embedding budget; the tail beyond this is not indexed. Production Tier-1
# deployments typically raise this to 8-12; the code default is 4.
# RAG_EMBEDDING_MAX_CHUNKS="4"

# --- OpenAI (LLM + pgvector embeddings) ---------------------------------------
# OPENAI_API_KEY=""                     # credential; the LLM plane and the
#                                       # pgvector embedder refuse without it
# OPENAI_BASE_URL="https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions"   # gateway override
# OPENAI_MODEL="gpt-4o-mini"
# OPENAI_TIMEOUT_MS="15000"
# OPENAI_TEMPERATURE="0.3"
# OPENAI_MAX_TOKENS="512"
# Plane one is above: the upload is chunked, embedded and retrieved by
# `searchDocuments` when a live turn is drafted. Plane two is the ElevenLabs
# agent's OWN `knowledge_base` list — what the agent on the call cites without a
# vector query. Today that list holds the six shipped disposition-policy documents
# declared in agent/securevoice.agent.yaml (ELEVENLABS_KB_EN/AR/HI/UR/FR/SW), one
# per spoken language, with their text in agent/policy/.
#
# With this on, a document that reaches READY is ALSO published to the platform as
# a text document built from the text the chunker already produced (the PDF is not
# parsed twice) and attached to the agent. Idempotent per row: the vendor document
# id is stored on Document.platformDocId, so a retry PATCHes that document instead
# of stacking a second copy of one policy onto the agent.
#
# OFF unless this exact value is set. It is a write to a shared vendor resource that
# outlives the request, so it follows the same rule as ELEVENLABS_COMMERCIAL_USE and
# TWILIO_LIVE_SEND: an explicit "true", and anything else — including an empty
# string — means off. A deployment that never sets it behaves exactly as before.
# Needs ELEVENLABS_API_KEY and an agent: the tenant's own Organization.elevenAgentId
# when it has one, otherwise ELEVENLABS_AGENT_ID. Flag on with neither is a refusal
# recorded on the row (platformError), never a silent pass.
#
# MULTI-TENANT HAZARD, stated because the fallback cannot state it itself: if several
# organisations share the one ELEVENLABS_AGENT_ID, each tenant's published policy
# becomes retrievable on the others' calls. Bind an agent per organisation
# (Organization.elevenAgentId) before turning this on anywhere that hosts more than
# one tenant.
# KB_PLATFORM_SYNC="false"
# Ceiling on the text one platform document carries. Over the ceiling the publish is
# REFUSED and recorded — half a policy quoted to a frightened cardholder is worse
# than none, and the tenant's own RAG path is unaffected either way.
# KB_PLATFORM_MAX_CHARS="200000"

# --- Cloudflare R2 (object storage — call recordings, knowledge documents) ---
# R2 is S3-compatible. The storage module (src/lib/storage/s3.ts) reads these
# env vars and returns null when they are not set — an unconfigured deployment
# degrades to "no object store" rather than throwing.
#
# To set up R2:
#   1. Create a bucket in the Cloudflare dashboard (R2 → Buckets).
#   2. Create an R2 API token (R2 → Manage R2 API Tokens) with
#      Object Read & Write permissions on that bucket.
#   3. Copy the values below into .env (NOT .env.example — that file is
#      committed and must never carry a real credential).
#
# The endpoint is the R2 endpoint for your account, which you can find in the
# R2 dashboard under "Account ID" — it looks like:
#   https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
#
# Region is always "auto" for R2. The storage module requires it to be set
# explicitly (it does not default to "auto") because a silent wrong region
# produces a SignatureDoesNotMatch at the far end that nobody can read.
#
# R2_ENDPOINT="https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com"
# R2_REGION="auto"
# R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID="<r2-api-token-access-key-id>"
# R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY="<r2-api-token-secret-access-key>"
# R2_BUCKET="<bucket-name>"
#
# BOTH HALVES NEED THEM. The app container reads this file through `env_file`, and
# it is the app's webhook that archives a recording; the retention worker declares
# no env_file, so docker-compose.yml passes the same five values to it explicitly.
# Configuring one service and not the other is how a deployment stores a customer's
# voice past the 30-day tier — the writer wired, the deleter not — and the sweep
# reports `audioStore: "none-configured"` while the bucket keeps growing.
#
# Optional: force path-style URLs (needed for some S3-compatible proxies).
# R2_FORCE_PATH_STYLE="true"

# --- Telecom adapter (optional carrier abstraction) ---------------------------
# When set, plain outbound calls and SMS to a number go through the formal
# carrier adapter (src/lib/adapters/telecom-adapter.ts) instead of the direct
# Twilio path. Valid values: twilio, infobip, sip (alias: sip-trunk).
#
# This is a FAST-PATH for simple calls only. A call that carries inline TwiML,
# a media stream, a speech context, or a tenant-specific caller ID always uses
# the direct path — see the adapterFastPathApplies header in src/lib/twilio.ts.
# The adapter cannot carry those, and inventing a way to pass them would mean
# the adapter starts knowing what a bank says to a customer.
#
# TELECOM_ADAPTER=""
# Credentials the adapter reads for the carrier it selects
# (src/lib/adapters/telecom-adapter.ts). With TELECOM_ADAPTER=infobip or
# "sip", the send REFUSES (typed 401/422) naming the missing knob — setting the
# adapter without these is loud, not silent.
# INFOBIP_API_KEY=""                    # required; refusal names it
# INFOBIP_FROM=""                       # sender for voice AND sms legs
# INFOBIP_BASE_URL="https://api.infobip.com"   # gateway/proxy override
# INFOBIP_TIMEOUT_MS="10000"            # per request
# SIP_TRUNK_ORIGINATION_URL=""          # the trunk endpoint placeCall dials
# SIP_TRUNK_USERNAME=""                 # trunk auth (pair, not either alone)
# SIP_TRUNK_PASSWORD=""
# SIP_TRUNK_FROM=""                     # caller ID the trunk presents
# SIP_TRUNK_TIMEOUT_MS="10000"          # per request

# --- Compliance flags ---
# All three are server-enforced and CANNOT be disabled by a route or client.
# Leaving them "true" is the safe default; they exist so that the policy is
# visible in code review, not because anyone should turn them off.
# These are now backed by the feature-flag registry (src/lib/flags.ts) as
# FEATURE_COMPLIANCE_DISCLOSURE, FEATURE_COMPLIANCE_NO_SECRETS and
# FEATURE_PII_REDACTION respectively. Either env name is accepted; the flag
# reader checks both.
COMPLIANCE_OPENING_DISCLOSURE="true"
COMPLIANCE_NO_CREDENTIAL_REQUESTS="true"
COMPLIANCE_PII_REDACTION="true"

# --- Paystack (test keys only, opt-in) ---
# PAYSTACK_SECRET_KEY=""
# PAYSTACK_BASE_URL="https://api.paystack.co"   # override for a proxy/gateway
# PAYSTACK_MAX_ATTEMPTS="3"                    # max retry attempts
# PAYSTACK_BACKOFF_BASE_MS="250"               # base backoff in ms
# PAYSTACK_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS="10000"          # per-request HTTP timeout
# PAYSTACK_ALLOW_LIVE_KEYS=1                   # opt in to live (sk_live_) keys

# --- Supabase: NO SUPABASE_* VARS EXIST IN THIS PRODUCT -----------------------
# This section used to document SUPABASE_URL, SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY,
# SUPABASE_DATABASE_URL and NEXT_PUBLIC_-prefixed counterparts of the first
# two. Nothing read any of them — not src/, not scripts/, not
# mini-services/realtime, not compose, not Caddy. They were the residue of a
# deleted bank-side PostgREST scaffold (tests/contracts/money-case-audit.test.ts
# rule 3 pins that scaffold's deletion and its env names are example strings in
# that test, not reads), and the "required only by current-user-avatar" comment
# was false: that component was adapted to Better Auth and imports no Supabase
# client (`@supabase/*` is not even a dependency).
#
# Supabase IS this deployment's production Postgres (docs/VPS-RECOVERY.md) —
# reached through DATABASE_URL at the top of this file (IPv4 pooler, sslmode +
# the committed anchor). Setting names from this deleted section produces
# nothing: the provisioning trap was following the docs and getting silence.
# Do not re-add SUPABASE_* without a call site that reads it.

# --- Groq (optional fast LLM reply layer) ---
# When set, the agent drafts replies via Groq (qwen/qwen3.8-27b default (llama-3.1 retired from Groq 2026-09),
# override with GROQ_MODEL) in the customer's language under voice guardrails
# (<= 50 words, no markdown/emojis, never break character). Intent routing +
# compliance guardrails stay deterministic. Unset â†’ scripted replies.
GROQ_API_KEY=""
GROQ_MODEL="qwen/qwen3.8-27b"
# Optional — Gemini fallback, used only when neither GROQ_API_KEY nor
# LITELLM_API_KEY is set. Same precedence: first configured provider wins.
GEMINI_API_KEY=""
GEMINI_MODEL="gemini-1.5-flash"

# --- Langfuse (LLM observability) ---
# Traces every LLM call (model, tokens, latency, errors) to the Langfuse
# dashboard. Optional — when unset the app runs without tracing and the
# voice path is unaffected. Keys are from cloud.langfuse.com → Settings → API Keys.
# LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY=""
# LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY=""
# LANGFUSE_BASE_URL="https://cloud.langfuse.com"

# --- LiteLLM proxy (optional, recommended for production) ---------------------
# Optional, and INERT UNTIL WIRED — read this before setting the key.
#
# Provider precedence is GROQ_API_KEY > LITELLM_API_KEY > GEMINI_API_KEY: the
# first configured one wins, so setting LITELLM_API_KEY only adds a FALLBACK rung
# (src/lib/llm.ts:162 builds it, providerLadder orders it after groq).
#
# Measured on the production box 2026-10-18, because "recommended for production"
# was written before anyone checked whether it could be satisfied here:
#   · the host has no GPU, so the default model below cannot be self-hosted on it;
#   · the only live inference key present is GROQ_API_KEY, and it serves
#     qwen/qwen3.8-27b (HTTP 200) but 404s llama-3.3-70b-versatile;
#   · so pointing the proxy at Groq makes rung 2 share rung 1's vendor, key, rate
#     limit and model — it fails in exactly the outage a fallback exists for, and
#     adds an 8s hop to a customer's dead air.
# Wiring it properly therefore needs an upstream DISTINCT from groq, plus a
# registered model on the proxy. The compose service is behind the `llm-proxy`
# profile until then, because unprofiled it boot-looped 7230 times (current
# litellm exits without a master key). See docs/GAP-REGISTER.md §41.
#
# Two traps that will bite whoever sets the key, both read from the code:
#   · LITELLM_BASE_URL must be the FULL completions path. The adapter POSTs to
#     baseUrl verbatim (src/lib/adapters/llm-adapter.ts:282), so
#     http://litellm:4000/v1/chat/completions works and a bare host 404s. The
#     config default of http://localhost:4000 is doubly wrong in compose: it lacks
#     the path, and localhost is not the proxy from inside another container.
#   · docker-compose sets LITELLM_BASE_URL to an EMPTY default for
#     voice-stream-worker, and the config getter uses `??`, so "" beats the
#     default rather than falling through to it. Set it explicitly.
#   · the `app` service passes no LITELLM_* at all, so the console ladder cannot
#     see this key until compose passes it. That is deliberate: the voice rung was
#     the one being built.
# codebase deliberately has NO OpenAI provider: voice turns are latency-budgeted
# at ~2s, and customer transcripts are bank-customer PII that should stay inside
# the deployment's own boundary rather than a third party that trains on it.
#
# LiteLLM is the path that satisfies both at once: point it at a self-hosted
# model (or any upstream you already have a contract with) and the manifest
# still pulls in zero inference SDKs.
#
#   docker run -p 4000:4000 ghcr.io/berriai/litellm --model 'llama-3.3-70b-instruct'
#
# Default model is llama-3.3-70b-instruct: chosen for real coverage of the
# languages this agent speaks (ar, hi, ur, fr, sw) and for being permissively
# licensed so a proxy can serve it on commodity GPUs. Neither holds on the current
# production host, which has no GPU — see the block above.
LITELLM_API_KEY=""
# The adapter POSTs to this value verbatim (src/lib/adapters/llm-adapter.ts:282),
# so it has to be the FULL completions path. The note here used to read
# "host:port of the proxy, NOT a /v1 path", which 404s every call, and the config
# default (http://localhost:4000) is wrong twice over under compose: no path, and
# localhost is not the proxy from inside another container.
LITELLM_BASE_URL="http://litellm:4000/v1/chat/completions"
LITELLM_MODEL="llama-3.3-70b-instruct"
LITELLM_TIMEOUT_MS="8000"
LITELLM_TEMPERATURE="0.3"
LITELLM_MAX_TOKENS="160"

# --- LLM adapter plane (src/lib/adapters/llm-adapter.ts) ----------------------
# Read ONLY by the generic adapter, which is not the live voice path: the
# conversation plane keeps its own precedence (GROQ > LITELLM > GEMINI above),
# and this codebase's no-OpenAI-by-default rule for CUSTOMER TRANSCRIPTS still
# holds — wiring OPENAI here routes adapter-plane calls, not dial replies.
# Each knob is a fallback under per-call config; unset defers to the default.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=""                     # credential; adapter refuses without it
# OPENAI_BASE_URL="https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions"   # gateway override
# OPENAI_MODEL="gpt-4o-mini"
# OPENAI_TIMEOUT_MS="15000"
# OPENAI_TEMPERATURE="0.3"
# OPENAI_MAX_TOKENS="512"
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=""
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL="https://api.anthropic.com/v1/messages"
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL="claude-3-5-sonnet-latest"
# ANTHROPIC_TIMEOUT_MS="20000"
# ANTHROPIC_TEMPERATURE="0.3"
# ANTHROPIC_MAX_TOKENS="1024"

# --- Realtime push + reverse proxy -------------------------------------------
# The Command Center's live feed uses a websocket when this is wired up, and falls
# back to its SSE endpoint when it isn't — so the console works either way.

# HMAC key shared by the app and mini-services/realtime. Generate with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# Leave empty to disable the push path entirely: the realtime service refuses every
# connection and the console stays on SSE.
REALTIME_INGEST_SECRET=""

# Where the app sends signed ingest. Inside compose this is the service name
# (the compose default is already http://realtime:4000 — leave this UNSET so
# the default wins). NEVER localhost/127.0.0.1: in the app container that
# resolves to the app itself and silently kills the push path.
# REALTIME_URL="http://realtime:4000"

# Comma-separated browser origins the socket accepts. Empty = any (dev only).
# WHO READS THIS: mini-services/realtime/src/server.ts (the socket's Origin
# check at handshake) — NOT src/lib/config.ts; the app never validates its own
# env here. docker-compose.yml passes it through to the realtime service with a
# https://$SITE_ADDRESS default. The name is the socket's; leave it be only if
# the socket runs.
REALTIME_ALLOWED_ORIGIN=""

# --- Edge / canonical origin ---------------------------------------------------
# The app's OWN canonical hostname: src/lib/site-origin.ts promotes it to https
# for canonical links and the sitemap, and src/worker/dial.ts builds the Twilio
# media-stream URL from it. NOT a Caddy setting — Caddy serves the literal site
# list in `Caddyfile` (+ untracked `sites.d/*.caddy`, see sites.d/README.md).
SITE_ADDRESS=""
# ACME contact for certificate-expiry notices (the one Caddy env var left).
# WHO READS THIS: the Caddyfile (`email {$ACME_EMAIL:...}`), fed to the caddy
# service by docker-compose.yml. No application code reads it.
ACME_EMAIL=""

# --- Edge layer ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Pre-auth request budget per client IP, applied in src/proxy.ts before routing.
# Deliberately separate from RATE_LIMIT_PER_HOUR, which governs expensive API calls.
EDGE_RATE_LIMIT_PER_HOUR="600"

# Liveness/readiness probes (`/api/health`, `/api/readyz`) run on their own bucket,
# because the edge budget is sized for a browser and a monitor is not a browser.
# Set below the monitoring interval and Caddy's `health_uri` starts collecting 429s,
# the deployment looks unhealthy to whoever is watching it, and nothing is wrong —
# which is what happened on the VPS before this bucket existed. `probe-budget.test.ts`
# couples this number to the Caddyfile's health_uri so the two cannot drift apart.
PROBE_RATE_LIMIT_PER_HOUR="6000"

# The shared secret ElevenLabs generates for the post-call webhook endpoint;
# used to verify the ElevenLabs-Signature: t/v0 header (WP-4).
ELEVENLABS_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""

# --- Outbound bank webhook (WP-5) ---
# HMAC-SHA256(secret, {t}.{body}) -> SV-Signature header. The bank verifies with the
# reference implementations in scripts/verify_sv_signature.{ts,py}.
BANK_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""
BANK_WEBHOOK_URL=""

# --- Overage billing (see docs/UNIT-ECONOMICS.md) ------------------------------
# A bank buys a bundle of included interventions per commitment period; usage
# above that is rated per unit and invoiced after the period closes.
#
# Overage rate OVERRIDE, in integer minor units (fils/cents) per billable unit.
# Never a float.
#
# UNSET ⇒ EACH PLAN'S OWN published rate applies — Starter 15¢, Growth 12¢, as
# /pricing quotes them, read from `PLANS[].overageCents` in src/lib/commercial.ts.
# Enterprise publishes no rate (its volume beyond the bundle is negotiated), so
# the rater REFUSES to bill it rather than inventing a figure. An org whose plan
# cannot be resolved at all falls back to the published default of 15.
#
# SET ⇒ it overrides every plan, including Enterprise. That is the point of the
# knob: one switch for a deployment mid-dispute. It also means the page's per-tier
# figures no longer describe what you charge, so set it deliberately.
#
# SET BUT NOT A POSITIVE INTEGER ⇒ the rater REFUSES (rate_not_configured). A typo
# such as "0.15" in this integer field is the dangerous case: defaulting silently
# would hide the misconfiguration, and billing a fractional rate would corrupt the
# integer arithmetic. Fix the value; do not delete the line to make the refusal go
# away.
#
# $0.15/unit against the ~$0.05 blended Twilio + ElevenLabs + model cost of one
# intervention is a 66% gross margin on overage.
OVERAGE_RATE_MINOR_PER_UNIT=""
# THE INVOICING SWITCH. "1" lets src/worker/overage.ts raise and push overage
# invoices. ANY OTHER VALUE, INCLUDING UNSET, means the worker rates every
# tenant, reports `would_bill`, and writes NOTHING.
#
# This is the difference between "the arithmetic is provably right against a real
# closed period" and "a bank receives a document nobody checked". Turn it on
# after reading one run's `would_bill` amounts against the tenant's own ledger.
# It is not enough on its own: the Paddle adapter separately refuses a live key
# unless PADDLE_ALLOW_LIVE_KEYS=1, and invoices are manual-collection (Net-30)
# documents — Merchant of Record holds no card authorization to charge later.
OVERAGE_INVOICING_ENABLED=""
# How often the billing worker re-rates closed periods (default 24). Overdue by
# hours, not by design: the invoice reference is a function of (org, period), so
# a re-run never bills the same window twice.
# OVERAGE_SWEEP_HOURS="24"
# Fallback included allowance, used only for an org with no usable Paddle
# subscription row. A subscribed org gets its plan's allowance instead:
# Starter 500, Growth 2,500, Enterprise 10,000 — DERIVED from
# `PLANS[].includedPerMonth` by src/lib/payments/plans.ts, not copied by hand.
INCLUDED_UNITS_PER_PERIOD=""
# ISO 4217. Compared case-insensitively, stored upper-case.
BILLING_CURRENCY="AED"
# NOTE — there is NO Paddle price to create for overage, and deliberately so.
# The overage is raised as a MANUAL-COLLECTION transaction carrying a custom
# line price: `items[].price = { amount_minor, currency_code }` with
# `quantity = <billable units>` (see createInvoice in src/lib/payments/paddle.ts
# and pushOverageInvoiceToGateway in src/lib/payments/overage.ts). Paddle has no
# "overage" concept in its catalog to fill in, and a custom line is how a
# negotiated per-unit amount is expressed — Paddle rejects an unknown price_id,
# so inventing a catalog entry to carry the amount would be the wrong direction.
#
# What the billing entity still needs: a PADDLE_API_KEY with transaction-write
# permission, and a configured notification destination
# (PADDLE_WEBHOOK_SECRET), because the invoice is created against your account.

# --- Judge demo shortcut (published credential, by design) ---------------------
# The one-click demo login renders only when BOTH are set. If either is empty the
# button is replaced with a message rather than a button that fails.
#
# The demo account is a PUBLISHED credential — that is the point of a judge
# shortcut — so it must be simulation-only, capped, and rate-limited. It is NOT
# an operator account. There is deliberately no one-click operator login: an
# operator credential written into the client bundle is a credential published
# to every visitor, and this one could place real intervention calls.
# THESE MUST REACH THE BUILD, NOT JUST THE RUNTIME. `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` is inlined
# into the client bundle by the Next.js compiler during `bun run build`, so
# setting them in a container's `environment:` does nothing for this feature.
# They are wired as docker build args (Dockerfile + docker-compose.yml, all seven
# services built from that Dockerfile) and sourced from THIS file. If they are
# missing at build time the button is not hidden — it is compiled out of the
# bundle, and no amount of restarting the container or setting them at runtime
# will bring it back. That is why a deploy can be green, healthy, and still show
# a judge nothing but a sign-in form.
#
# This is deliberately NOT the same treatment as BETTER_AUTH_SECRET, which stays a
# build arg that is never inlined and never reaches the client. One is a
# published demo credential; the other is a real secret.
#
# PRODUCTION GATE: this pair must be EMPTY on any sovereign/production profile.
# The seeded demo seat is org owner of `demo-bank` with a funded credit wallet,
# and `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` is inlined into the browser bundle — so a non-empty value
# is a published org-owner login. Empty compiles the one-click button out of the
# bundle entirely, which is the intended production posture.
NEXT_PUBLIC_DEMO_LOGIN_EMAIL=""
NEXT_PUBLIC_DEMO_LOGIN_PASSWORD=""

# --- Rate limiting — shared backend -------------------------------------------------
# REDIS_URL above (line ~218) also selects the rate-limit backend: when it is set,
# `installRateLimitStoreFromEnv()` swaps the in-process token bucket for a shared
# one backed by the same Redis, enforced atomically in Lua. When it is unset, the
# limiter stays per-process and every restart clears every bucket — on a
# multi-instance deployment that is `capacity x instances`, and the boot log says
# `[ratelimit] store installed  store=memory`. See src/lib/ratelimit.ts.
# No separate variable: one Redis, one URL, one thing to misconfigure.

# Webhook signing DEMO (/api/webhooks)
# The secret that endpoint returns so an integrator can exercise a receiver.
# Leave empty for a per-process random value that rotates on boot.
# NEVER set this to WEBHOOK_SECRET: this value is returned in a response body.
WEBHOOK_DEMO_SECRET=

# --- Cloudflare Turnstile (bot gate on sign-in) ----------------------------------
# The SITE key is public by construction — it is inlined into the client bundle
# and is meant to be, exactly like the demo credential above. The SECRET key is
# not: it is read only on the server, in src/lib/compliance/turnstile.ts, which
# redeems the token against Cloudflare's siteverify endpoint. Never give it a
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
#
# Unset both => no widget renders and the server treats Turnstile as "not
# enforced". That is a deliberate switch so a self-hosted evaluation box with no
# Cloudflare account still boots and still signs people in — it is NOT safe for
# production. Set both in production.
#
# The hostname allowlist is the second half of the check and is easy to forget:
# with the secret set but no allowlist, every token fails the hostname comparison
# and the sign-in looks broken for a reason nothing on the page explains. List
# the PUBLIC hostnames only. A production value must never contain `localhost`
# or `127.0.0.1`, or a token minted on a developer's machine would satisfy a
# production check.
NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=""
TURNSTILE_SECRET=""
TURNSTILE_HOSTNAMES=""

